== a last statement with no terminator ==
  0: select - [read] 1:1-1:10
     <select 1;>
  1: select - [read] 2:1-2:9
     <select 2>
== a file that is only comments ==
  (no statements)
== an empty file ==
  (no statements)
== blank space between statements belongs to neither ==
  0: select - [read] 1:1-1:10
     <select 1;>
  1: select - [read] 6:1-6:10
     <select 2;>
== an unterminated string swallows the rest, as the engine would ==
  0: select - [read] 1:1-2:10
     <select 'oops;
select 2;>
== and so does an unterminated block comment ==
  (no statements)
== a bare terminator is not a statement ==
  0: select - [read] 2:1-2:10
     <select 1;>
== a comment BEFORE a statement is not part of it ==
  0: select - [read] 3:1-3:10
     <select 1;>
== a comment AFTER one, on its own line, is not either ==
  0: select - [read] 1:1-1:10
     <select 1;>
